**Theorem 10.2 (no uniformly finite supplement).** For every authentic
$P$ and every finite set $Z$, no encoding $z$ and exact deterministic
update of Definition 10.1 exist on $\mathcal C_P$. No nondeterministic
realization with its exact projected response sets exists either. For
each fixed $N$, either exact contract requires at least $N$
supplemental values already on $\{x_{N,m}:m<N\}$.

Proof. If $|Z|<N$, Theorem 9.2 gives $N$ starts with identical full
$O^+$. Two different $m,m'$ therefore have equal $z$ and equal
$\eta$. Under a deterministic update, induction on the common row
index forces equal augmented states, hence equal $O^+$ successors,
throughout both actual scan continuations. The first differing RD of
Theorem 9.2 contradicts this conclusion. This argument permits $z$ to
change at every later cut and permits arbitrarily expensive updates;
neither helps after two starts have merged.

Under the nondeterministic contract the same augmented start has one
set of length-$D_{P,N}$ projected responses. Exactness at each of the
two concrete starts would make this set two different singletons,
again contradicting Theorem 9.2. The same argument for every pair
forces injectivity of $m\mapsto z(x_{N,m})$. Finally choose
$N>|Z|$ on the fixed common $P$ to obtain both uniform impossibilities.
No reachability from a newly chosen source or new installation is
used. ∎

Permissive inclusion does not have this consequence. For example, the
relation containing precisely the projected edges
$(O^+(s),O^+(Fs))$ for $s\in\mathcal C^-_P$ contains every actual
chart path. From a common starting projection it contains all the
displayed different projected paths, whereas each concrete start has
only its own prescribed path. Such an overapproximation fails the
exact-response contract. It is not an alternative interpretation of
original admission or of an original refusal.

